<h1>Toni Brooks</h1>
<p>Candidate for Mayor, Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027</p>
<p><a href="https://www.brooksforchicago.com" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website</a></p>
<p><a href="https://www.brooksforchicago.com/platforms" rel="nofollow noopener">Toni Brooks&#39;s campaign has published one platform piece so far, the Chicago Audit &amp; Exchange, and says more is coming. Tap here to read it on her site, then come back to comment.</a></p>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>The platform</h3>
<p>The idea: Audit City Hall for money sitting unused and move it into neighborhoods, without raising taxes.</p>
<p>First example: Eliminate 500 to 700 long-vacant city positions to free $45 million, then give 12,000 family caregivers a $3,500 credit, off property taxes for owners and off rent for renters.</p>
<p>Still to come: Three pillars called Dignity, Dollars, and Defense are announced but not yet published.</p>
<h3>Next to the other candidates</h3>
<p>Only Brooks: A tax credit for family caregivers.</p>
<p>Cash to households: Carlisle backs guaranteed income, Cardenas proposes $3,000 yearly stipends for working families, and Brooks proposes a $3,500 credit for family caregivers.</p>
<p>Similar approach: Quigley, Mendoza, and Cardenas also look for money inside the existing budget, through a savings report, a waste program called TRIM, and a new Auditor General.</p>
<p>Not covered so far: Police, housing, schools, transit, and pensions, which most other candidates address.</p>
<h2>the more perfect platform</h2>
